# 109: Improve Update Command in AzerothCore Script

## Status
- [ ] Open
- [ ] In Progress
- [x] Completed

## Current Behavior

The `scripts/azerothcore` script has these modes:
- `install {core|modules|all}` - Clones and builds (wipes source first)
- `update` - Always pulls and rebuilds, even if no changes

Problems:
1. No way to rebuild without pulling (for first compile after clone)
2. Always rebuilds even when nothing changed (wastes 30+ minutes)
3. Legacy `scripts/update-2` has outdated hardcoded paths

## Intended Behavior

Improve `update` command to:
1. Check for remote changes before rebuilding
2. Only pull and rebuild if changes exist
3. Support `--force` flag to rebuild without remote changes
4. Validate source exists before attempting build

Usage: `MAKE_JOBS=8 source scripts/azerothcore && azerothcore update [--force]`

## Suggested Implementation Steps

1. Add --force flag parsing to update-azerothcore
2. Use git fetch + rev-parse to compare local vs remote HEAD
3. Only proceed with pull+rebuild if changes detected or --force
4. Add source directory validation
5. Remove deprecated scripts/update-2

## Related Documents

- scripts/azerothcore (main script)
- scripts/update-2 (deprecated, removed)
- issues/108-thread-count-variable (MAKE_JOBS requirement)

## Notes

This issue was discovered while working on issue 101 (verify server startup). The
source was already cloned but binaries weren't compiled, and there was no clean
way to just build without re-cloning.

Initially planned to add a separate `build` mode, but user feedback indicated that
`update --force` is the cleaner approach - two commands (install/update) instead
of three.

